  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Generative genomics accurately predicts future experimental results (2025) in bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory). Community discussion on: substack (1).</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Generative genomics accurately predicts future experimental results (2025) in bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory) is authored by Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh and Vaishali Marar. Topics: Generative grammar, Genomics, Computer science, Artificial intelligence and Generative model.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">It is discussed on Substack (1 mention). 1 online mentions.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 preprint is already drawing early attention beyond its citation record: it has one online mention, one downstream reuse signal, and a scite record that currently registers a single mentioning citation. The visible discussion is still sparse, but it points to interest in the paper\u2019s claim that generative genomics can predict experimental outcomes in advance.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Generative genomics accurately predicts future experimental results presents an AI framework designed to model biological experiments in a way that mirrors how experiments are planned and executed in the lab or clinic. In the abstract, the authors describe GEM-1 as a generative system intended to capture the diversity of bulk and single-cell gene expression data, positioning the work within current efforts to make biomedical AI both flexible and experimentally useful.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article is a 2025 bioRxiv preprint from a Bio-Synthesis research team, and its current impact profile is early-stage. OpenAlex records no citations yet, so the scholarly citation trail has not begun to accumulate. Even so, the work is not entirely quiet: the digest shows one online mention, a single Substack discussion, and one documented downstream reuse signal in Europe PMC, indicating that the paper is beginning to circulate in biomedical discourse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">That pattern suggests interest that is broader than formal citation counts alone. The one Substack mention points to public-facing engagement with the paper\u2019s framing of \u201cbiological foundation models,\u201d while the Europe PMC reuse signal indicates the record is already being picked up in a biomedical indexing context. Scite also shows one mentioning citation, which is consistent with a very early and still thin evidence base.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record shows a nascent but visible reception: the paper is attracting discussion, has at least one downstream reuse signal, and is being noticed in biomedical literature infrastructure. At this stage, the evidence supports cautious language about emerging attention rather than established scholarly influence.</p>",
